Hi Karl,

For the chr_lengths argument in scan1perm, does it matter if I use the genetic map vs. the physical map? Or do I have to use a specific one?

Thanks,
Basel

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Sun May  3 12:07:34 2020
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Sun, 03 May 2020 12:07:34 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl2-disc] gmap vs pmap for permutations
Message-ID: <D2AdDgtKr5w.ToaU3ioKAQAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <D2AdDgtKr5w.MfXs1XRkBgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <D2AdDgtKr5w.MfXs1XRkBgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/D2AdDgtKr5w/m/ToaU3ioKAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It matters, as in they're different, but you could use either one.

chr_lengths is used only if you're getting autosome- and X-chromosome-specific threshold, in which case the lengths are used as weights. See Broman et al. (2006) https://doi.org/10.1534/genetics.106.061176

I would use the genetic lengths, because the distribution of the maximum LOD score on a chromosome depends on genetic rather than physical length.

karl

